1
00:00:00,112 --> 00:00:00,462
Part one.

2
00:00:00,875 --> 00:00:02,288
Everything is a meme stock.

3
00:00:02,712 --> 00:00:04,525
Steven Bartlett asks him directly:

4
00:00:04,838 --> 00:00:05,925
do you think we're in a bubble?

5
00:00:06,338 --> 00:00:07,212
Cuban says yes.

6
00:00:07,638 --> 00:00:09,038
But not like the dot-com bubble.

7
00:00:09,450 --> 00:00:09,925
This time,

8
00:00:10,362 --> 00:00:12,650
he says, the problem is that everything has

9
00:00:12,662 --> 00:00:13,812
become a meme stock.

10
00:00:14,238 --> 00:00:14,588
Bitcoin.

11
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:15,475
Micron.

12
00:00:15,888 --> 00:00:17,625
Even giants like Google and Apple.

13
00:00:18,038 --> 00:00:19,850
His evidence is how prices move.

14
00:00:20,262 --> 00:00:22,450
A company can miss its earnings forecast by

15
00:00:22,462 --> 00:00:23,288
a single penny,

16
00:00:23,600 --> 00:00:25,362
and the stock drops eighteen percent.

17
00:00:25,788 --> 00:00:28,075
The real value of that business didn't change

18
00:00:28,088 --> 00:00:29,725
by eighteen percent overnight.

19
00:00:30,150 --> 00:00:32,763
Buyers and sellers just rushed in and out

20
00:00:32,775 --> 00:00:33,438
on the news.

21
00:00:33,838 --> 00:00:34,812
In Cuban's words,

22
00:00:35,050 --> 00:00:37,075
we're trading stocks like baseball cards.

23
00:00:37,500 --> 00:00:39,788
There's no intrinsic value in the price anymore.

24
00:00:40,200 --> 00:00:41,350
Only perceived value,

25
00:00:41,625 --> 00:00:43,138
and a guess about what the next person

26
00:00:43,150 --> 00:00:43,575
will pay.

27
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:45,500
That phrase is worth a pause,

28
00:00:45,712 --> 00:00:48,450
because Cuban literally started his career with baseball

29
00:00:48,462 --> 00:00:48,938
cards.

30
00:00:49,350 --> 00:00:50,700
At nine or ten years old,

31
00:00:51,062 --> 00:00:52,388
he bought packs for a quarter,

32
00:00:52,688 --> 00:00:53,525
repackaged them,

33
00:00:53,950 --> 00:00:55,412
and sold them for fifty cents.

34
00:00:55,825 --> 00:00:57,000
So what pops this bubble?

35
00:00:57,438 --> 00:00:58,525
Here's the surprising part.

36
00:00:58,938 --> 00:01:00,312
Cuban says he has no idea.

37
00:01:00,725 --> 00:01:02,600
Even Bartlett admits it's tempting to put a

38
00:01:02,612 --> 00:01:04,062
few dollars into Nvidia.

39
00:01:04,475 --> 00:01:05,200
Cuban gets it,

40
00:01:05,463 --> 00:01:08,462
because this technology really is without precedent.

41
00:01:08,875 --> 00:01:09,962
What he does know is history.

42
00:01:10,375 --> 00:01:11,850
When the internet bubble burst,

43
00:01:12,100 --> 00:01:14,125
Dell and Amazon got crushed.

44
00:01:14,550 --> 00:01:16,962
People mocked Amazon for losing so much money.

45
00:01:17,375 --> 00:01:18,862
Today, it's worth trillions.

46
00:01:19,275 --> 00:01:21,175
Anyone who stuck it out did very well.

47
00:01:21,588 --> 00:01:22,775
So there will be winners again,

48
00:01:23,138 --> 00:01:23,712
he says.

49
00:01:24,138 --> 00:01:25,450
He just can't tell you which ones.

50
00:01:25,862 --> 00:01:26,662
Is it Anthropic?

51
00:01:27,075 --> 00:01:27,850
Is it OpenAI?

52
00:01:28,288 --> 00:01:28,850
He doesn't know.

53
00:01:29,262 --> 00:01:30,712
And that uncertainty matters,

54
00:01:30,888 --> 00:01:33,988
because underneath this AI boom sits something much

55
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:35,775
less exciting than chatbots.

56
00:01:36,188 --> 00:01:36,450
Debt.

57
00:01:36,862 --> 00:01:37,350
A lot of it.
